The user's team pointed at the wrong device entirely. Let me confirm the fault is fully localised to router4 by cross-checking the evidence one final time:

- **Router3**: healthy — has routes to both 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24 (Location A) and to 192.168.200.0/24 (Location C).
- **Router4**: only one connected route (192.168.200.0/24); eth4 (→ router5, 192.168.15.2/24) and eth5 (→ router3, 172.25.1.1/24) were **DOWN since startup** (`link ups: 0`, `link downs: 1` at 13:08:11).
