=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies entirely on router4, not router3 as the ticket asserts. Router4 has
two uplink interfaces — eth4 (192.168.15.2/24, connecting toward router5 via
link_r4_r5) and eth5 (172.25.1.1/24, connecting toward router3 via link_r4_r3) —
that were administratively brought down at lab startup. Both interfaces had their
IP addresses correctly configured but were never raised: `ip link show` showed the
flags `<BROADCAST,MULTICAST>` without UP, "link ups: 0" and "link downs: 1" at
startup time, with no connected route for either subnet appearing in the kernel FIB.

Because those two interfaces were down, router4 could not send or receive RIP
updates on either of its WAN-side links. Its entire RIP table showed every learned
route at metric 16 (infinity), and its routing table contained exactly one entry:
the directly-connected 192.168.200.0/24 (Location C). PC11–14 use router4
(192.168.200.1) as their default gateway; when they send traffic toward Location A
(172.16.10.x / 172.16.20.x), router4 has no route and drops the packets silently.

Router3 is completely healthy: it has valid RIP-learned routes to both Location A
prefixes (metric 4 via router2) and to Location C (metric 2 via router4's eth5),
and its own interfaces are up. The fault is not a router3 hardware failure; it is
router4's eth4 and eth5 being administratively shut down (injected as `ip link set
ethX down` in the startup).

Corrective fix: on router4, run `ip link set eth4 up && ip link set eth5 up` (already
executed during this investigation — both interfaces came UP immediately, confirming
the physical Kathara segments were intact). Allow 30–90 seconds for RIP to reconverge,
after which router4 will install routes to all remote prefixes and PC11–14 will
regain full reachability to Location A.
=== END ===
